      "text": "To find the total surface area of the pyramid, we need to calculate the area of the base and the areas of the four triangular faces.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the area of the base. The base is a square with side length 10 cm, so the area \\(A_{\\text{base}}\\) is:\n\\[\nA_{\\text{base}} = 10 \\times 10 = 100 \\text{ cm}^2\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the area of one of the triangular faces. Each triangular face is an isosceles triangle with a base of 10 cm and a height that we need to determine. The height of the triangular face is the slant height of the pyramid. To find this, we can use the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangle formed by the height of the pyramid, half the length of the base of the pyramid, and the slant height.\n\nThe height of the pyramid is 12 cm, and half the length of the base is \\( \\frac{10}{2} = 5 \\) cm. Let \\( s \\) be the slant height. Then:\n\\[\ns = \\sqrt{12^2 + 5^2} = \\sqrt{144 + 25} = \\sqrt{169} = 13 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\nSo, the area of one triangular face \\( A_{\\text{triangle}} \\) is:\n\\[\nA_{\\text{triangle}} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 \\times 13 = 65 \\text{ cm}^2\n\\]\nSince there are four triangular faces, the total area of the four triangular faces \\( A_{\\text{triangles}} \\) is:\n\\[\nA_{\\text{triangles}} = 4 \\times 65 = 260 \\text{ cm}^2\n\\]\n\nFinally, the total surface area \\( A_{\\text{total}} \\) of the pyramid is the sum of the area of the base and the area of the four triangular faces:\n\\[\nA_{\\text{total}} = A_{\\text{base}} + A_{\\text{triangles}} = 100 + 260 = 360 \\text{ cm}^2\n\\]\n\nThus, the total surface area of the pyramid is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{360}\n\\]",
