    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the total surface area of a right pyramid with a square base. The total surface area of the pyramid is the sum of the area of the square base and the areas of the four triangular faces.\n\n1. **Calculate the area of the square base:**\n   The side length of the square base is 10 cm.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of the square base} = 10 \\times 10 = 100 \\text{ cm}^2\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the slant height of the triangular faces:**\n   The peak of the pyramid is 12 cm above the center of the base. This forms a right triangle with the slant height of the triangular face and half the side length of the base. The half-side length of the base is:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{10}{2} = 5 \\text{ cm}\n   \\]\n   Using the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangle formed by the slant height (let's call it \\( l \\)), the half-side length (5 cm), and the height of the pyramid (12 cm):\n   \\[\n   l = \\sqrt{12^2 + 5^2} = \\sqrt{144 + 25} = \\sqrt{169} = 13 \\text{ cm}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the area of one triangular face:**\n   Each triangular face has a base of 10 cm and a slant height of 13 cm. The area of one triangular face is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of one triangular face} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{slant height} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 \\times 13 = 65 \\text{ cm}^2\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the total area of the four triangular faces:**\n   Since there are four triangular faces, the total area of the four triangular faces is:\n   \\[\n   4 \\times 65 = 260 \\text{ cm}^2\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the total surface area of the pyramid:**\n   The total surface area is the sum of the area of the square base and the total area of the four triangular faces:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total surface area} = 100 + 260 = 360 \\text{ cm}^2\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the total surface area of the pyramid is \\(\\boxed{360}\\) square centimeters.",
